---
title: "Paged output"
lang: en
---

This is the corpus pin for the print/PDF track. It carries the shapes a paged rendering is
judged on, so that `taliesin pdf paged.tmd` exercises every one of them:

- a **cross-reference to a figure further down the document**, which must resolve to a real
  page number rather than to zero (the signature of resolving before pagination settled),
- **more than one figure**, so the generated list of figures is not degenerate,
- **enough prose to force real page breaks**, so running heads and folios have somewhere to
  appear.

The PDF is a *rendering of this HTML*. There is no second compiler path, no `.tex`
intermediate and no format-specific emitter: the same document you are reading in the
browser is laid out into pages by the browser itself.

## Figures and forward references

The scree plot is @fig-scree; the reconstruction is @fig-recon. On paper each of those
carries the page it lives on, which is the one thing a printed cross-reference needs and a
web one does not.

![A scree plot of the component variances.](scree.png){#fig-scree}

The reference above @fig-recon is the interesting case: it points *forwards*, to a figure
that has not been laid out yet at the moment the reference is read. Resolving it correctly
requires the page numbers to be known, which is why the driver waits for pagination to
finish before capturing rather than printing on load.

## A section that spans a page break

The prose below exists to push the second figure onto a later page. Without it every
reference would resolve to the page it was written on, and the feature would look like it
worked while proving nothing.

A paged document is judged on details a scrolling one never has to answer for: whether a
heading is stranded alone at the foot of a page, whether a single line of a paragraph is
left behind when the rest moves on, whether a figure is torn in half by a page boundary, and
whether the reader can tell where they are without counting. Each of those is one CSS
declaration here, and each is invisible until it is missing.

Widow and orphan control is the least visible of them and the most felt. A paragraph that
leaves one line at the bottom of a page reads as a mistake even to someone who could not
name what is wrong, which is why the floor here is three lines rather than the browser
default of two.

Hyphenation is the other half of that. Justified or not, a narrow measure with no
hyphenation opens rivers of whitespace between words; with it, the line breaks fall where a
typesetter would put them. It needs the document's language to choose a dictionary, which is
why this document declares `lang: en` in its front matter and why a print stylesheet that
sets `hyphens: auto` without one is a rule that never fires.

Running heads answer the other question: a reader who opens the document in the middle
should be able to tell which section they are in without paging backwards. That is what the
italic line at the top of this page is doing, and it is generated from the section heading
rather than written by hand.

![The reconstruction at four ranks.](recon.png){#fig-recon}

## What is deliberately not here

Whole-book output, an index, and publishing a PDF alongside a built site are all out of
scope for this pin. Each would need its own corpus document, and this one is kept to the
shapes the single-document track actually renders.
